" (https://proceedings.mlr.press/v164/mandlekar22a/mandlekar22a.pdf)\nciteturn9view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 13; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v164/mandlekar22a/mandlekar22a.pdf\",\"lineno\":272}); Total lines: 595\nL242@P5: demo subsets corresponding to the mixture of the previous subsets. Similar data mixtures have been\nL243@P5: used for evaluations in batch RL [18]. Appendix B shows the average trajectory lengths in each data\nL244@P5: subset – lower quality datasets contain demonstrations that take more time to solve the task.\nL245@P5: BC-RNN is a strong baseline on suboptimal human data, but there is room for improvement.\nL246@P5: Table 2 shows that BC exhibits a large performance gap between the Better and Worse 100-demo\nL247@P5: subsets (roughly 27% and 35% for Can and Square respectively). Interestingly, BC-RNN is able\nL248@P5: to nearly eliminate this gap in performance on the Can task, but not on the Square task. However,\nL249@P5: BC-RNN outperforms BC on all datasets (7%-35% improvement). Comparing results on the 100\nL250@P5: Better demonstrations and 100 Okay demonstrations to the 200 Worse-Better demonstrations and 200\nL251@P5: Worse-Okay demonstrations further allows us to analyze how adding 100 “worse” demonstrations\nL252@P5: impacts the performance of each algorithm. Most algorithms decline in performance while BC-RNN\nL253@P5: is able to uniformly improve from the added data. Comparing the performance of BC-RNN on the\nL254@P5: 200-demo Square mixture datasets (55.3%, 73.3%, 74.0%) to the high-quality 200-demo Square (PH)\nL255@P5: dataset (84.0%) shows that there is still room for algorithms to improve on the use of this data.\nL256@P5: Diagnostic dataset shows that Batch RL struggles in simpler settings as well. The final row of\nL257@P5: Table 2 shows additional results on a diagnostic dataset termed Can-Paired, where a single operator\nL258@P5: collected 2 demonstrations for each of 100 task initializations – one successful demonstration, and\nL259@P5: one where the can is tossed outside of the bin (task failure), for a total of 200 demonstrations. There\nL260@P5: is a strong expectation for batch RL algorithms to be able to distinguish between actions leading\nL261@P5: to successful placement and actions leading to task failure, but even in this simple setting, most\nL262@P5: algorithms suffer, providing a pessimistic view of the state-of-the-art. The 5% improvement that IRIS\nL263@P5: provides over BC-RNN suggests that introducing history-dependence into state-of-the-art batch RL\nL264@P5: algorithms might be a promising direction for future work.\nL265@P5: 4.3 Effect of Observation Space (C5)\nL266@P5: Learning from image observations can match low-dim agent performance. In Table 3, we\nL267@P5: present policy learning results when using image observations instead of ground-truth object locations\nL268@P5: – an important setting for real-world policy learning. BC-RNN still maintains superior performance\nL269@P5: improvements over BC on the complex Square and Transport tasks, and with the exception of\nL270@P5: Transport (MH), maintains nearly the same performance as learning from ground-truth observations.\nL271@P5: This result provides an optimistic view for learning with real-world raw sensory observations.\nL272@P5-6: 6Features used for robot proprioception can matter. In Fig 2a, we study the effect of adding end ef\u0002fector velocities to the observations (+ EEF Vel), and joint positions and velocities to the observations\nL273@P6: (+ Joint). Surprisingly, we find that including end effector velocity information, and joint information\nL274@P6: hurts agents trained on low-dim observations substantially (49%-88% relative performance drop),\nL275@P6: while image-based agents are more tolerant to the inclusion of this extra information (2%-29%\nL276@P6: relative performance drop). We hypothesize that performance drops might be due to overfitting\nL277@P6: to the presence of this extra information not needed for solving these tasks. Thus, practitioners\nL278@P6: should take care to engineer the robot observation space and exclude possibly irrelevant information –\nL279@P6: information-hiding can be a powerful paradigm for training proficient robots [44].\nL280@P6: Image randomization and wrist observations can be crucial for manipulation tasks. In Fig 2a,\nL281@P6: we report performance drops from removing pixel shift image randomization (- Rand) and the wrist\nL282@P6: camera (- Wrist) from image-based agents to understand their importance. We see that removing\nL283@P6: randomization results in 47% and 35% relative performance drops on Square and Transport respec\u0002tively, and removing wrist images results in 9% and 43% relative drops. Consequently, both wrist\nL284@P6: camera images and image randomization play a substantial role in producing performant policies. We\nL285@P6: confirm the importance of each for visuomotor imitation in the real world as well (see Sec 4.7). Wrist\nL286@P6: observations likely help the robot improve gripper alignment for grasping and randomization helps\nL287@P6: the policy develop invariance for portions of the image that are not important for action prediction.\nL288@P6: 4.4 Effect of Hyperparameter Choice (C5)\nL289@P6: In this section, we take our default hyperparameters for BC-RNN and study the effect of changing a\nL290@P6: subset of them to report practical recommendations for learning from human datasets (see Appendix I\nL291@P6: for BCQ and CQL). We present our results in Fig 2b (low-dim) and Fig 2c (image).\nL292@P6: (larger LR) Increasing the learning rate from 1e-4 to 1e-3 affects the performance of image-agents\nL293@P6: substantially (drop of 35%-63%), while low-dim agents are more tolerant to the change. (no GMM)\nL294@P6: Using a deterministic policy instead of learning a GMM action distribution results in significant\nL295@P6: relative performance drops on the MH datasets (especially low-dim Transport, with a drop of 58%).\nL296@P6: (larger MLP) Using a larger MLP size at each RNN timestep reduces performance uniformly,\nL297@P6: suggesting that it is possible to overfit to dataset actions if network architectures are too large.\nL298@P6: (shallow Conv) Using a shallow convolutional network [45] instead of the ResNet backbone [46] for\nL299@P6: encoding image observations reduces performance significantly – with relative drops of 25%-62%,\nL300@P6: suggesting that large-capacity visual encoders are crucial for visuomotor imitation. (smaller RNN\nL301@P6: dim) Reducing the size of the RNN hidden dimension from 400 to 100 (low-dim) and 1000 to 400\nL302@P6: (image) uniformly decreases performance (drops of 3%-58%), showing the importance of a large\nL303@P6: RNN hidden dimension. (Recommendations) We recommend tuning the LR (especially for image\nL304@P6: agents) and network structure (MLP size, size of RNN dim) carefully. Opting to use a GMM policy\nL305@P6: and a ResNet encoder appears to be uniformly better.\nL306@P6: 4.5 Selecting a Policy to Evaluate (C4)\nL307@P6: Model selection in offline policy learning can be challenging – for this reason, in our simulation\nL308@P6: experiments, we evaluated every policy checkpoint online and reported the best one. This is not\nL309@P6: feasible for real-world settings, making offline policy selection desirable. In Fig 4a, we show that this\nL310@P6: can be non-trivial, by showing the relative performance drop when selecting the policy using the best\nL311@P6: loss on validation data (common in supervised learning), and when using the final training checkpoint\nL312@P6: as well (common in offline RL [47, 18, 27]) – in both cases, the selected policy is significantly worse\nL313@P6: than the best one (10% to 100% decrease). See Appendix G for more detailed results and discussion.\nL314@P6: This motivates the need for better offline evaluation metrics.\nL315@P6: 4.6 Effect of Dataset Size (C3)\nL316@P6: To study how dataset size impacts performance, we formed smaller 20% and 50% subsets of our\nL317@P6: human datasets by sampling trajectories. We evaluate low-dim and image BC-RNN agents across\nL318@P6: these subsets in Table 27 and Table 28. There are several promising results here. We first note that\nL319@P6: less complex tasks (Lift, Can) can yield proficient policies (75%-100% success rate) using a small\nL320@P6: fraction of the data (20%). Second, while policies trained on more complex tasks (Square, Transport)\nL321@P6: suffer substantially when using 50% or 20% of the data, the converse is also true – adding more data\nL322@P6-7: 7(e.g. moving from 20% to 50% or 50% to 100% size) can result in significant policy improvement.\nL323@P7: This confirms the value of using large human datasets as a means to obtain proficient policies for\nL324@P7: challenging and complex manipulation tasks.\nL325@P7: 4.7 Applicability to Real-World Settings\nL326@P7: Here, we show that design decisions made in simulation can potentially transfer to real world settings.\nL327@P7: We collected 3 additional real-world datasets with a Franka robotic arm – Lift (Real), Can (Real), and\nL328@P7: Tool Hang (Real). Each consists of 200 trajectories collected by one operator. We train BC-RNN and\nL329@P7: report the final policy checkpoint success rate, over 30 rollouts, due to the time-consuming nature\nL330@P7: of real world policy evaluation. We also emphasize that no real-world hyperparameter tuning took\nL331@P7: place, so our results are a lower bound. We were able to train proficient Lift (96.7%) and Can\nL332@P7: (73.3%) policies, and the Tool Hang (3.3%) policy is able to generate some task successes, despite\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2019/file/947018640bf36a2bb609d3557a285329-Paper.pdf)\nciteturn9view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 12; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2019/file/947018640bf36a2bb609d3557a285329-Paper.pdf\",\"lineno\":12}); Total lines: 733\nL0@P0: Causal Confusion in Imitation Learning\nL1@P0: Pim de Haan∗1, Dinesh Jayaraman†‡, Sergey Levine†\nL2@P0: ∗Qualcomm AI Research, University of Amsterdam,\nL3@P0: †Berkeley AI Research, ‡ Facebook AI Research\nL4@P0: Abstract\nL5@P0: Behavioral cloning reduces policy learning to supervised learning by training a dis\u0002criminative model to predict expert actions given observations. Such discriminative\nL6@P0: models are non-causal: the training procedure is unaware of the causal structure of\nL7@P0: the interaction between the expert and the environment. We point out that ignoring\nL8@P0: causality is particularly damaging because of the distributional shift in imitation\nL9@P0: learning. In particular, it leads to a counter-intuitive “causal misidentification”\nL10@P0: phenomenon: access to more information can yield worse performance. We inves\u0002tigate how this problem arises, and propose a solution to combat it through targeted\nL11@P0: interventions—either environment interaction or expert queries—to determine the\nL12@P0: correct causal model. We show that causal misidentification occurs in several\nL13@P0: benchmark control domains as well as realistic driving settings, and validate our\nL14@P0: solution against DAgger and other baselines and ablations.\nL15@P0: 1 Introduction\nL16@P0: Imitation learning allows for control policies to be learned directly from example demonstrations\nL17@P0: provided by human experts. It is easy to implement, and reduces or removes the need for extensive\nL18@P0: interaction with the environment during training [58, 41, 4, 1, 20].\nL19@P0: However, imitation learning suffers from a fundamental problem: distributional shift [9, 42]. Training\nL20@P0: and testing state distributions are different, induced respectively by the expert and learned policies.\nL21@P0: Therefore, imitating expert actions on expert trajectories may not align with the true task objective.\nL22@P0: While this problem is widely acknowledged [41, 9, 42, 43], yet with careful engineering, naïve\nL23@P0: behavioral cloning approaches have yielded good results for several practical problems [58, 41, 44,\nL24@P0: 36, 37, 4, 33, 3]. This raises the question: is distributional shift really still a problem?\nL25@P0: In this paper, we identify a somewhat surprising and very problematic effect of distributional shift:\nL26@P0: “causal misidentification.” Distinguishing correlates of expert actions in the demonstration set from\nL27@P0: true causes is usually very difficult, but may be ignored without adverse effects when training and\nL28@P0: testing distributions are identical (as assumed in supervised learning), since nuisance correlates\nL29@P0: continue to hold in the test set. However, this can cause catastrophic problems in imitation learning\nL30@P0: due to distributional shift. This is exacerbated by the causal structure of sequential action: the very\nL31@P0: fact that current actions cause future observations often introduces complex new nuisance correlates.\nL32@P0: To illustrate, consider behavioral cloning to train a neural network to drive a car. In scenario A, the\nL33@P0: model’s input is an image of the dashboard and windshield, and in scenario B, the input to the model\nL34@P0: (with identical architecture) is the same image but with the dashboard masked out (see Fig 1). Both\nL35@P0: cloned policies achieve low training loss, but when tested on the road, model B drives well, while\nL36@P0: model A does not. The reason: the dashboard has an indicator light that comes on immediately when\nL37@P0: the brake is applied, and model A wrongly learns to apply the brake only when the brake light is on.\nL38@P0: Even though the brake light is the effect of braking, model A could achieve low training error by\nL39@P0: misidentifying it as the cause instead.\nL40@P0: 1Work mostly done while at Berkeley AI Research.\nL41@P0-1: 33rd Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2019), Vancouver, Canada.Scenario A: Full Information Scenario B: Incomplete Information\nL42@P1: policy attends to brake indicator\nL43@P1: Figure 1: Causal misidentification: more information yields worse imitation learning performance. Model A\nL44@P1: relies on the braking indicator to decide whether to brake. Model B instead correctly attends to the pedestrian.\nL45@P1: This situation presents a give-away symptom of causal misidentification: access to more information\nL46@P1: leads to worse generalization performance in the presence of distributional shift. Causal misidentifi\u0002cation occurs commonly in natural imitation learning settings, especially when the imitator’s inputs\nL47@P1: include history information.\nL48@P1: In this paper, we first point out and investigate the causal misidentification problem in imitation\nL49@P1: learning. Then, we propose a solution to overcome it by learning the correct causal model, even when\nL50@P1: using complex deep neural network policies. We learn a mapping from causal graphs to policies,\nL51@P1: and then use targeted interventions to efficiently search for the correct policy, either by querying an\nL52@P1: expert, or by executing selected policies in the environment.\nL53@P1: 2 Related Work\nL54@P1: Imitation learning. Imitation learning through behavioral cloning dates back to Widrow and Smith,\nL55@P1: 1964 [58], and has remained popular through today [41, 44, 36, 37, 4, 13, 33, 56, 3]. The distributional\nL56@P1: shift problem, wherein a cloned policy encounters unfamiliar states during autonomous execution,\nL57@P1: has been identified as an issue in imitation learning [41, 9, 42, 43, 25, 19, 3]. This is closely tied\nL58@P1: to the “feedback” problem in general machine learning systems that have direct or indirect access\nL59@P1: to their own past states [47, 2]. For imitation learning, various solutions to this problem have been\nL60@P1: proposed [9, 42, 43] that rely on iteratively querying an expert based on states encountered by some\nL61@P1: intermediate cloned policy, to overcome distributional shift; DAgger [43] has come to be the most\nL62@P1: widely used of these solutions.\nL63@P1: We show evidence that the distributional shift problem in imitation learning is often due to causal\nL64@P1: misidentification, as illustrated schematically in Fig 1. We propose to address this through targeted\nL65@P1: interventions on the states to learn the true causal model to overcome distributional shift. As we\nL66@P1: will show, these interventions can take the form of either environmental rewards with no additional\nL67@P1: expert involvement, or of expert queries in cases where the expert is available for additional inputs. In\nL68@P1: expert query mode, our approach may be directly compared to DAgger [43]: indeed, we show that we\nL69@P1: successfully resolve causal misidentification using orders of magnitude fewer queries than DAgger.\nL70@P1: We also compare against Bansal et al. [3]: to prevent imitators from copying past actions, they train\nL71@P1: with dropout [53] on dimensions that might reveal past actions. While our approach seeks to find\nL72@P1: the true causal graph in a mixture of graph-parameterized policies, dropout corresponds to directly\nL73@P1: applying the mixture policy. In our experiments, our approach performs significantly better.\nL74@P1: Causal inference. Causal inference is the general problem of deducing cause-effect relationships\nL75@P1: among variables [52, 38, 40, 50, 10, 51]. “Causal discovery” approaches allow causal inference from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2019/file/947018640bf36a2bb609d3557a285329-Paper.pdf)\nciteturn9view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 12; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2019/file/947018640bf36a2bb609d3557a285329-Paper.pdf\",\"pattern\":\"previous action\"}); Total lines: 733\nL120@P2: with distributional shift when deployed: actions At are chosen by the imitator rather than the expert,\nL121@P2: affecting the distribution of Z\nL122@P2: t\nL123@P2: and Xt. This in turn affects the policy mapping from Xtto At,\nL124@P2: leading to poor performance of expert-cloned policies. We define “causal misidentification\" as the\nL125@P2: phenomenon whereby cloned policies fail by misidentifying the causes of expert actions.\nL126@P2: 3.1 Robustness and Causality in Imitation Learning\nL127@P2: Intuitively, distributional shift affects the relationship of the expert action Atto nuisance variables,\nL128@P2: but not to the true causes. In other words, to be maximally robust to distributional shift, a policy\nL129@P2: must rely solely on the true causes of expert actions, thereby avoiding causal misidentification. This\nL130@P2: intuition can be formalized in the language of functional causal models (FCM) and interventions [38].\nL131@P2: Functional causal models: A functional causal model (FCM) over a set of variables {Yi}\nL132@P2: n\nL133@P2: i=1\nL134@P2: is a tuple (G, θG) containing a graph G over {Yi}\nL135@P2: n\nL136@P2: i=1, and deterministic functions fi(·; θG) with\nL137@P2: parameters θG describing how the causes of each variable Yi determine it: Yi = fi(YPa(i;G), Ei; θG),\nL138@P2: where Eiis a stochastic noise variable that represents all external influences on Yi, and Pa(i; G)\nL139@P2: denote the indices of parent nodes of Yi, which correspond to its causes.\nL140@P2: An “intervention” do(Yi) on Yito set its value may now be represented by a structural change in this\nL141@P2: graph to produce the “mutilated graph” GY¯i, in which incoming edges to Yi are removed.1\nL142@P2: Applying this formalism to our imitation learning setting, any distributional shift in the state Xt may\nL143@P2: be modeled by intervening on Xt, so that correctly modeling the “interventional query” p(At|do(Xt))\nL144@P2: is sufficient for robustness to distributional shifts. Now, we may formalize the intuition that only a\nL145@P2: policy relying solely on true causes can robustly model the mapping from states to optimal/expert\nL146@P2: actions under distributional shift.\nL147@P2: In Appendix B, we prove that under mild assumptions, correctly modeling interventional queries does\nL148@P2: indeed require learning the correct causal graph G. In the car example, “setting” the brake light to on\nL149@P2: or off and observing the expert’s actions would yield a clear signal unobstructed by confounders: the\nL150@P2: brake light does not affect the expert’s braking behavior.\nL151@P2: 3.2 Causal Misidentification in Policy Learning Benchmarks and Realistic Settings\nL152@P2: Before discussing our solution, we first present several testbeds and real-world cases where causal\nL153@P2: misidentification adversely influences imitation learning performance.\nL154@P2: Control Benchmarks. We show that causal misidentification is induced with small changes to\nL155@P2: widely studied benchmark control tasks, simply by adding more information to the state, which\nL156@P2: intuitively ought to make the tasks easier, not harder. In particular, we add information about the\nL157@P2: previous action, which tends to correlate with the current action in the expert data for many standard\nL158@P2: control problems. This is a proxy for scenarios like our car example, in which correlates of past actions\nL159@P2: 1\nL160@P2: For a more thorough overview of FCMs, see [38].\nL161@P2-3: 3are observable in the state, and is similar to what we might see from other sources of knowledge about\nL162@P3: the past, such as memory or recurrence. We study three kinds of tasks: (i) MountainCar (continuous\nL163@P3: states, discrete actions), (ii) MuJoCo Hopper (continuous states and actions), (iii) Atari games: Pong,\nL164@P3: Enduro and UpNDown (states: two stacked consecutive frames, discrete actions).\nL165@P3: (a) Pong (b) Enduro (c) UpNDown\nL166@P3: Figure 3: The Atari environments with indicator of\nL167@P3: past action (white number in lower left).\nL168@P3: For each task, we study imitation learning in\nL169@P3: two scenarios. In scenario A (henceforth called\nL170@P3: \"CONFOUNDED\"), the policy sees the augmented ob\u0002servation vector, including the previous action. In\nL171@P3: the case of low-dimensional observations, the state\nL172@P3: vector is expanded to include the previous action\nL173@P3: at an index that is unknown to the learner. In the\nL174@P3: case of image observations, we overlay a symbol\nL175@P3: corresponding to the previous action at an unknown\nL176@P3: location on the image (see Fig 3). In scenario B\nL177@P3: (\"ORIGINAL\"), the previous action variable is replaced with random noise for low-dimensional ob\u0002servations. For image observations, the original images are left unchanged. Demonstrations are\nL178@P3: generated synthetically as described in Appendix A. In all cases, we use neural networks with\nL179@P3: identical architectures to represent the policies, and we train them on the same demonstrations.\nL180@P3: Fig 4 shows the rewards against varying demonstration dataset sizes for MountainCar, Hopper, and\nL181@P3: Pong. Appendix E shows additional results, including for Enduro and UpNDown. All policies are\nL182@P3: trained to near-zero validation error on held-out expert state-action tuples. ORIGINAL produces rewards\nL183@P3: tending towards expert performance as the size of the imitation dataset increases. CONFOUNDED either\nL184@P3: requires many more demonstrations to reach equivalent performance, or fails completely to do so.\nL185@P3: Overall, the results are clear: across these tasks, access to more information leads to inferior perfor\u0002mance. As Fig 11 in the appendix shows, this difference is not due to different training/validation\nL186@P3: losses on the expert demonstrations—for example, in Pong, CONFOUNDED produces lower validation\nL187@P3: loss than ORIGINAL on held-out demonstration samples, but produces lower rewards when actually\nL188@P3: used for control. These results not only validate the existence of causal misidentification, but also\nL189@P3: provides us with testbeds for investigating a potential solution.\nL190@P3: Real-World Driving. Our testbeds introduce deliberate nuisance variables to the “original”\nL191@P3: observation variables for ease of evaluation, but evidence suggests that misattribution is pervasive\nL192@P3: in common real-world imitation learning settings. Real-world problems often have no privileged\nL193@P3: “original” observation space, and very natural-seeming state spaces may still include nuisance factors—\nL194@P3: as in our dashboard light setting (Fig 1), where causal misattribution occurs when using the full image\nL195@P3: from the camera.\nL196@P3: In particular, history would seem a natural part of the state space for real-world driving, yet\nL197@P3: recurrent/history-based imitation has been consistently observed in prior work to hurt performance,\nL198@P3: thus exhibiting clear symptoms of causal misidentification [36, 56, 3]. While these histories contain\nL199@P3: valuable information for driving, they also naturally introduce information about nuisance factors\nL200@P3: such as previous actions. In all three cases, more information led to worse results for the behavioral\nL201@P3: cloning policy, but this was neither attributed specifically to causal misidentification, nor tackled\nL202@P3: using causally motivated approaches.\nL203@P3: Metrics → Validation Driving Performance\nL204@P3: Methods ↓ Perplexity Distance Interventions Collisions\nL205@P3: HISTORY 0.834 144.92 2.94 ± 1.79 6.49 ± 5.72\nL206@P3: NO-HISTORY 0.989 268.95 1.30 ± 0.78 3.38 ± 2.55\nL207@P3: Table 1: Imitation learning results from Wang et al. [56].\nL208@P3: Accessing history yields better validation performance,\nL209@P3: but worse actual driving performance.\nL210@P3: We draw the reader’s attention to particularly\nL211@P3: telling results from Wang et al. [56] for learning\nL212@P3: to drive in near-photorealistic GTA-V [24] envi\u0002ronments, using behavior cloning with DAgger\u0002inspired expert perturbation. Imitation learn\u0002ing policies are trained using overhead image\nL213@P3: observations with and without “history” infor\u0002mation (HISTORY and NO-HISTORY) about the\nL214@P3: ego-position trajectory of the car in the past.\nL215@P3: Similar to our tests above, architectures are identical for the two methods. And once again, like in\nL216@P3: our tests above, HISTORY has better performance on held-out demonstration data, but much worse\nL217@P3: performance when actually deployed. Tab 1 shows these results, reproduced from Wang et al. [56]\nL218@P3: Table II. These results constitute strong evidence for the prevalence of causal misidentification in\nL219@P3-4: 40.1 0.3 0.5 1 5\nL220@P4: Demonstration dataset size (x 1000)\nL221@P4: 200\nL222@P4: 180\nL223@P4: 160\nL224@P4: 140\nL225@P4: 120\nL226@P4: 100\nL227@P4: Reward\nL228@P4: (a) MountainCar\nL229@P4: 0.1 0.5 1 5 20 100 300\nL230@P4: Demonstration dataset size (x 1000)\nL231@P4: 0\nL232@P4: 500\nL233@P4: 1000\nL234@P4: 1500\nL235@P4: Reward\nL236@P4: (b) Hopper\nL237@P4: 10 200 250 800\nL238@P4: Demonstration dataset size (x 1000)\nL239@P4: 20\nL240@P4: 10\nL241@P4: 0\nL242@P4: 10\nL243@P4: 20\nL244@P4: Reward\nL245@P4: Scenario A (Confounded)\nL246@P4: Scenario B (Original)\nL247@P4: Expert\nL248@P4: (c) Pong\nL249@P4: Figure 4: Diagnosing causal misidentification: net reward (y-axis) vs number of training samples (x-axis) for\nL250@P4: ORIGINAL and CONFOUNDED, compared to expert reward (mean and stdev over 5 runs). Also see Appendix E.\nL251@P4: realistic imitation learning settings. Bansal et al. [3] also observe similar symptoms in a driving\nL252@P4: setting, and present a dropout [53] approach to tackle it, which we compare to in our experiments.\nL253@P4: Subsequent to an earlier version of this work, Codevilla et al. [8] also verify causal confusion in\nL254@P4: realistic driving settings, and propose measures to address a specific instance of causal confusion.\nL255@P4: 4 Resolving Causal Misidentification\nL256@P4: Recall from Sec 3.1 that robustness to causal misidentification can be achieved by finding the true\nL257@P4: causal model of the expert’s actions. We propose a simple pipeline to do this. First, we jointly learn\nL258@P4: policies corresponding to various causal graphs (Sec 4.1). Then, we perform targeted interventions to\nL259@P4: efficiently search over the hypothesis set for the correct causal model (Sec 4.2).\nL260@P4: 4.1 Causal Graph-Parameterized Policy Learning\nL261@P4: Figure 5: Graph-parameterized\nL262@P4: policy.\nL263@P4: In this step, we learn a policy corresponding to each candidate causal\nL264@P4: graph. Recall from Sec 3 that the expert’s actions A are based\nL265@P4: on an unknown subset of the state variables {Xi}\nL266@P4: n\nL267@P4: i=1. Each Xi\nL268@P4: may either be a cause or not, so there are 2\nL269@P4: n possible graphs. We\nL270@P4: parameterize the structure G of the causal graph as a vector of n\nL271@P4: binary variables, each indicating the presence of an arrow from Xk\nL272@P4: to A in Fig 2. We then train a single graph-parameterized policy\nL273@P4: πG(X) = fφ([X\nL274@P4:  G, G]), where\nL275@P4:  is element-wise multiplication,\nL276@P4: and [·, ·] denotes concatenation. φ are neural network parameters,\nL277@P4: trained through gradient descent to minimize:\nL278@P4: EG[`(fφ([Xi\nL279@P4:  G, G]), Ai)], (1)\nL280@P4: where G is drawn uniformly at random over all 2\nL281@P4: n graphs and ` is a mean squared error loss for the\nL282@P4: continuous action environments and a cross-entropy loss for the discrete action environments. Fig 5\nL283@P4: shows a schematic of the training time architecture. The policy network fφ mapping observations X\nL284@P4: to actions A represents a mixture of policies, one corresponding to each value of the binary causal\nL285@P4: graph structure variable G, which is sampled as a bernoulli random vector.\nL286@P4: In Appendix D, we propose an approach to perform variational Bayesian causal discovery over\nL287@P4: graphs G, using a latent variable model to infer a distribution over functional causal models (graphs\nL288@P4: and associated parameters)—the modes of this distribution are the FCMs most consistent with the\n"
